WEBVTT
TITLE Ip Fragmentation Cidr And Nat — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
An IPv4 address is 32 bits. A prefix is a cut position in those bits.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
With p bits fixed, the block holds 2 to the power 32 minus p addresses.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
A /27 has 32 addresses, so the block holding .144 starts at .128.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Network .128, broadcast .159, so the last assignable host is .158.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Every block has five numbers worth naming, and four of them are arithmetic.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
The mask is all ones in the prefix. Network equals address AND mask.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Usable hosts are 2 to the h minus 2: the all-zero and all-one host values are reserved.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
GATE 2019 Q28: with a /30 mask, .5 and .6 share a block and .2 does not.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
A router table is a list of prefixes. An entry is a candidate if the first p bits agree.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Several entries can match. The longest one wins, not the first one listed.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
GATE 2023 Q55: .118 matches /16, /19 and /24, so interface 3 answers.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
GATE 2024 Set 1: five hosts, twenty packets each, two hosts go via R2, so 40.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Two equal blocks merge exactly when they differ only in the bit just left of the prefix.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
Aggregation must not steal addresses. The merged block covers the union, no more.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
GATE 2022 Q45: 164.0/22 already covers 166.0/23, and 168.0/22 covers the other two.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
164 and 168 differ in the bit that a /21 would fix, so they stay two entries.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
MTU is the largest IP datagram a link carries, header included.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Every fragment but the last carries a multiple of 8 bytes of data.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
Each fragment repeats the identification, and sets its own length, offset and MF bit.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
Reassembly is at the destination only. A router may fragment again on the next link.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
GATE 2016 Set 1: 1000 bytes total, 100-byte MTU, 20-byte header.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
Twelve fragments carry 80 each, which is 960, and 20 bytes remain.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
Thirteen fragments. Offsets run 0, 10, 20 and up to 120 in units of 8.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
Three checks confirm any fragment list: sizes, offsets and the flag on the last one.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
GATE 2025 Set 2: 7488 bytes of UDP payload, plus 8 bytes of UDP header.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Five full fragments of 1480 leave 96 bytes for a sixth.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Six fragments, and the last is 96 plus 20, which is 116 bytes.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
The UDP header travels in the first fragment only, so losing any piece costs the whole segment.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
GATE 2024 Set 1: 1420 bytes total, then links of 542 and 360 bytes.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
The second link allows 336 bytes of data, so each fragment is cut again.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Six fragments reach the receiver, and their data still adds to 1400.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
Offsets stay relative to the original data, so the second cut continues from where it was.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
NAT rewrites the source address on the way out, using a table it keeps.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
With one public address, the port is what tells two private hosts apart.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
GATE 2024 Set 1 Q21: source IP and header checksum change; destination and length do not.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
The reply is matched on public address and port. No entry, no delivery.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
For any addressing question: find the block, then name which of the five numbers is wanted.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
For any fragmentation question: budget, floor to eight, then count.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Four slips account for most wrong answers in this clause.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
This clause connects backwards to routing and forwards to TCP.

